<article class="article" aria-label="Article">
<h2><span class="section-index">01 / THE PLAYER</span>Before the model, there was Larry Bird</h2>
<p class="lead">Larry Bird was one of basketball's great all-around forwards: a brilliant shooter, a demanding rebounder and a passer who seemed to see the next play before it existed.</p>
<p>Raised in French Lick, Indiana, Bird took Indiana State to the 1979 NCAA championship game before arriving in Boston. With the Celtics he won <strong>three NBA titles, three consecutive MVP awards (1984–86)</strong> and became one half of the defining 1980s rivalry with Magic Johnson. Those achievements made him a legend; the way he played explains why.</p>
<p>At 6-foot-9, Bird was a scorer who could run an offense without playing point guard. He punished a late closeout with his jumper, found cutters before defenders turned their heads and rebounded in traffic. He made the difficult look deliberate rather than spectacular. His shooting, vision and timing worked together, not as separate tricks.</p>
<p>We chose his <strong>1984–85 MVP season</strong>: 28.7 points, 10.5 rebounds and 6.6 assists per game, plus 42.7% shooting from three. Boston finished 63–19 and reached the NBA Finals. It was the kind of season that makes you ask what a modern version of Bird might look like.</p>
<h2><span class="section-index">02 / THE QUESTION</span>Can a modern player reproduce the shape of that season?</h2>
<p>We took Bird's <strong>1984–85 regular season</strong> and compared it against the Anticipate dataset of 279 NBA players from 2025–26. Each modern player cleared 1,000 regular-season minutes; traded players were counted once with combined season totals.</p>
<p>We scored seven features: points per 100 possessions, true shooting relative to that player's league, assist percentage, total rebound percentage, usage percentage, steal percentage and block percentage. A second component measures listed height and weight. A third applies a penalty when a player's broad position differs from Bird's.</p>
<p>The default weighting is <strong>70% statistical profile, 20% physical measurements and 10% positional group</strong>. We standardize differences against the same modern-player population and calculate a combined distance. A smaller number means a closer profile. It does <em>not</em> mean a player is a certain percentage as good as Bird.</p>
<p>The historical inputs are now checked against the published 1984–85 season: 34.3 points per 100 possessions, 25.7% assist share, 14.7% total rebound rate and 28.5% usage. Bird's 58.5% true shooting was 4.2 percentage points above the NBA's 54.3% average. He is listed at 206 centimetres and 220 pounds.</p>
<p>Three-point percentage is retained for editorial analysis but intentionally left out of the first model. That choice prevents Bird's low-attempt 1985 accuracy from being treated as equivalent to a modern high-volume perimeter role. It also gives the model a blind spot we need to discuss openly.</p>
</article>
