<div class="story-grid">
<article class="article">
<h2><span class="section-index">07 / WHAT THE EXPERIMENT TEACHES</span>The winner is stable. The meaning is not.</h2>
<p>Porter remained No. 1 under all three weighting schemes we checked: the original balanced model, a production-first version and a version placing greater weight on build and position. Johnson remained second. That stability makes the result harder to write off as an accident of the initial 70 / 20 / 10 formula.</p>
<p>But a stable answer is not a complete answer. The same limitations survive every weight change. A seven-variable production profile can recognize points, efficiency, usage, rebounds, assists and basic defensive activity, yet miss the very basketball qualities that explain how those numbers were created.</p>
<p>It cannot tell whether an assist came from a no-look touch pass, a straightforward kick-out or a designed handoff. It cannot see the defender who followed Bird into the corner and left a cutter open. It does not count a pass that causes the pass that earns the assist, or distinguish a rebound secured in traffic from one collected with little resistance. It has no direct measure of screens, defensive positioning, gravity, shot quality, anticipation or decision speed.</p>
<p>And the era is not a footnote. Bird played before modern spacing principles, before today's three-point volume and in a different defensive environment. Era-relative true shooting improves comparability; it does not turn 1985 into 2026.</p>
<p>The conclusion, then, is not that Michael Porter Jr. secretly <em>is</em> Larry Bird. It is that a model built to detect similar measurable seasons has a good reason to identify Porter, and that careful basketball analysis has a good reason to keep Johnson in the conversation.</p>
<blockquote>Similarity is a useful first question. It is not the final verdict.</blockquote>
<h2><span class="section-index">08 / THE VERDICT</span>There may not be a next Larry Bird. There are pieces of him.</h2>
<p>Bird's game was not a list of isolated skills. It was the combination of elite shooting, high-level passing, strong rebounding, positional size, timing, touch and the ability to move between those jobs without changing the team's rhythm. The value came from the package.</p>
<p>Porter resembles parts of the package that emerge in scoring, size and the way a shooting threat changes a defense. Johnson resembles parts that emerge when a forward rebounds, initiates and makes others better. Wagner brings a similar build, Avdija brings creation and LeBron brings the broad idea of an offense organized through a forward. None is a carbon copy, and none should be evaluated as though this ranking measures greatness.</p>
<p>Our published statistical answer is <strong>Michael Porter Jr.</strong> Our strongest stylistic counterargument is <strong>Jalen Johnson</strong>. The most honest basketball answer is that Bird's distinctiveness lives in the overlap between them, and in qualities our data cannot yet count.</p>
<p>That isn't a failure of the investigation. It is the reason to do one.</p>
</article>
